WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.210
Good morning, Hank! It's Tuesday. So I just turned 40, which you'll be unsurprised to learn has led to a bit of existential navel-gazing.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:11.370
But not with the conclusions that one might expect. So when I was younger, not just like in my teens,

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.599
but also through my twenties,

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:17.368
I thought of adulthood as like an ending. Like, in my imagination, human life was this epic road

00:00:17.368 --> 00:00:20.780
trip that started when you were born and ended when you reached your destination:

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.960
adulthood. And once you arrived at adulthood

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.280
you were never allowed to leave. To me, adulthood meant being weighed down with all these

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.520
responsibilities. You have to mow the lawn, and take your kids to soccer practice,

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:37.468
and pay your bills on time because you care about something called your credit score. And from what I could tell about adulthood, these

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:43.379
responsibilities would sort of pin you down and prevent you from growing or changing and you would just become a robot that fulfills

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:48.029
obligations. You know, I thought it was like an existential death that came several decades before your actual

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:52.649
physical death. So to delay the onset of adulthood and the metaphysical death that came with it,

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:57.089
my strategy in my twenties was to keep my life very light and lean. Like one time

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.050
I remember saying to a girl

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.219
I was dating, that if I needed to, I could pack my life up into my car in 15 minutes.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:06.899
She dumped me shortly thereafter. Now of course, I couldn't pack up my wall of memorabilia in 15 minutes,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.250
let alone my whole life. And of course,

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.580
I'm gonna tell you that I was underestimating adulthood because I have to, right?

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:15.180
Adults can't think of adulthood as a prison from which there is no earthly escape.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:21.119
We have to trick ourselves into thinking that we're having a great time and not at all being slowly strangled to death by homeowners

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.220
association meetings. And who knows? Maybe I am tricking myself, but for the record, I love being in a place

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.060
that's hard to leave.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.629
I like having roots in a place and friends I've known for 25 years.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:35.909
And that wasn't possible 20 years ago because, you know, I hadn't been alive for 25 years. Now sometimes I do feel

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:41.970
overwhelmed by the obligations of adulthood. But even so, I would not want to be 15 again or for that matter 25.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:46.499
I know that's easy for me to say because I've been incredibly lucky in both my professional and personal lives.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.759
But I know a lot of middle-aged people who are leading a wide variety of lives

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:53.819
and I honestly think that my experience is more the rule than the exception.

00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:56.140
Adulthood is so underrated

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:02.159
But more importantly, it turns out that adulthood is not a destination. Like I don't think my life has grown less

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:04.409
interesting as it has grown more settled.

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:10.409
I used to think that nothing stank of failure quite like living in the suburbs with your spouse and your two kids, that such an

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:15.219
existence was a tremendous waste of what Mary Oliver called "your one wild and precious life."

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.209
But I find at 40 that my life feels

00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:21.639
wilder and more precious than ever. That's not to say that my way of being an adult is the only good way

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.470
or that it's right for everyone. It's just that it's right for me. And if this is 40, I'll take it.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.610
Thanks so much to everybody for their birthday wishes and turtle cakes. Three more things. First: look, look, look, look, look!

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:32.420
I have finished signing all

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.809
200,000 copies! And the boxes are gone! Those

00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:39.429
200,000 sheets of paper are now at the printer in Virginia being bound into copies of "Turtles All The Way Down."

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.910
Amazon and barnesandnoble.com have both sold out of their allotment of signed copies,

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:47.169
but there is still lots of other ways to get them. More info at probablysignedturtles.com.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:51.160
Secondly, my video next week will be me reading the first chapter of  "Turtles All The Way Down."

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.959
That video will be over four minutes,

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:53.630
but that is not

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:59.409
punishable. There are precedents set in 2011 and 2008 when I read the first chapters of "The Fault In Our Stars" and "Paper Towns." And

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:02.859
lastly, I want to say to our friends and viewers affected by flooding in Texas and Louisiana

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:07.539
and also in India, Nepal, and Bangladesh that we are thinking of you. Nerdfighters, if you're able,

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.610
I hope you'll join me in donating to relief organizations. There are links in the Doobly-Doo below. Hank, I will see you on Friday.

